# Where the API is, as a PHONE sees it.
#
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_` works exactly like Vite's `VITE_`: everything under it is
# BAKED INTO THE BUNDLE when the bundle is built. It is not read at runtime, so
# setting it in the environment of whatever launches the app changes nothing.
# An APK carries the value that was in scope when its JavaScript was bundled,
# for as long as that APK exists.
#
# That is the detail worth writing down, because it decides where this belongs
# for an EAS build. An EAS build happens on Expo's machines, which never see
# this file, so a value here has no effect on the APK that comes back. It goes
# in the `env` block of the build profile in `eas.json`, one per profile,
# because a development build and a production build point at different hosts
# and the profile is the only place that distinction exists. This file is for
# `pnpm --filter @ariadna/mobile start` on this machine.
#
# It is public, and that is correct: the address of the API is not a
# credential. The credential is the session token, and it lives in the Android
# Keystore by way of `expo-secure-store`, never here.
#
# No trailing slash. The client builds every request as `<this>/items`, so a
# slash here produces `//items`.
#
# The default, http://127.0.0.1:3000, is loopback INSIDE the phone or the
# emulator, not on the machine running the API. It works in exactly two
# situations: an emulator with `adb reverse tcp:3000 tcp:3000`, or the Android
# emulator's host alias http://10.0.2.2:3000 in place of it. A real device on
# the same wifi needs the machine's LAN address. A device anywhere else needs
# the public hostname the Cloudflare Tunnel answers on, which is the same host
# as `ARIADNA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL`.
#
# Android 9 and up refuse cleartext HTTP by default, so a LAN or 10.0.2.2
# address over http:// needs `usesCleartextTraffic` in app.json for
# development. The tunnel's https:// hostname needs nothing.
EXPO_PUBLIC_ARIADNA_API_URL="http://127.0.0.1:3000"
